.TH CLOG_CLOSE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_close \- flush, close, and free a logger
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_close(clog logger);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Releases every resource that the logger owns.
  - The logger can sit on a file, and this can be the last handle that
    shares that file.  Every derived logger is then also closed, and
    this call closes the file descriptor.  When a derived logger still
    exists, the fd stays open and the call lowers the refcount of the
    file.  A handle that a second clog_open_file_mp(3) of the same
    rotating file returns shares that file in the same way.
  - Async logging can be on.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3).  When this is the
    last handle, the call drains everything that is already queued.  It
    joins the writer thread before it closes the file descriptor.  That
    drain is the last delivery attempt of the logger.  What it cannot
    deliver has no later retry, so the call writes a "log record
    truncated: undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): the
    logger closed" record in its place.
  - The logger can compress its rotated files.  See
    clog_open_file_mp(3).  When this is the last handle, the call waits
    until every compression that a rotation queued has finished.  It
    then stops the compression thread.  Every rotated file of the
    logger is therefore compressed, or left whole and uncompressed after
    a failed compression, by the time that the call returns.
  - It frees every key=value field that the logger holds.
  - It frees the internal write buffer.
  - It frees the state of this handle.  It frees the shared backing
    store only when it releases the last handle.

Do not use the handle after this call.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle to close.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Do not call clog_close() while another thread still makes any call with
that same handle.  Such a call is a log_* macro, clog_set_level,
clog_set_field and the functions beside it, clog_derive, or another
clog_close().  The caller must make sure that every other use of a
handle has stopped before it closes that handle.  This rule does not
cover a close that runs at the same time as a sibling handle that still
logs.  That sibling can be the root against a derived logger, or two
derived loggers.  Such a close stays safe, because the sibling is a
different handle that is still open.
For a logger that sits on an fd, from clog_open_fd_mp, this call does
NOT close the file descriptor.  The caller must do that.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_open_file_mp (3)
